#{[17592186410431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410431 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "903f2879-32c5-5f80-9994-a71eea52244c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410431 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4\\sqrt{b}}\\,$ without radicals in the denominator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9740bcef-2a83-552d-9db0-632ce64e6011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410432 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{b}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{b}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4\\sqrt{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4\\sqrt{b}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{b}}{\\sqrt{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b})\\sqrt{b}}{4(\\sqrt{b})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}-(\\sqrt{b})^{2}}{4(\\sqrt{b})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{ab}-b}{4b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(200,0.4),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X>90).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad031c31-3240-524a-8691-42d16ca618b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410433 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =80 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =120 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.4)=80\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.4)(1-0.4)=48.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(80,48).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>90).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>90)=P(X\\ge 91)≈P(Y>90.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>90.5) & =P(Z>\\frac{90.5-80}{\\sqrt{48}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.52) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.52) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.52) \\\\  & =1-0.9357 \\\\  & =0.0643\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>90)≈0.064.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0648.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $n$ where ${16}^{2n}={8}^{n+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6488fce-f74c-5334-a4ac-d8d71ef00e69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410434 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $16={2}^{4}$ and $8={2}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{4})}^{2n}={({2}^{3})}^{n+1}\\,\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{8n} & ={2}^{3(n+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8n & =3(n+1) \\\\ 8n & =3n+3 \\\\ 5n & =3 \\\\ n & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=6,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{5}({x}_{i})!=2.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc219d25-3086-59ef-9782-ded17eb3bf3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410435 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=6,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=2,$ and $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,3).$ \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb0efc72-9a29-58c3-a0f2-a7c2e7e96100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410436 #edb/ref 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,3) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3) \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(10)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0103\\,$ | $\\,0.0293\\,$ | $\\,0.0671\\,$ | $\\,0.1301\\,$ | $\\,0.2202\\,$ | $\\,0.3328\\,$ | $\\,0.4579\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1b12ff8-1521-511b-83f5-03f8cbdd4a50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410437 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 7)=P(X>6).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X>6) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =1-F(6) \\\\  & =1-0.1301 \\\\  & =0.8699.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1071 "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=17$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4.06.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=12.3,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb3ce7a3-f936-5690-b0b8-a114f6f8251b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the data set's mean.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=17,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{4.06}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=12.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{12.3-17}{\\sqrt{4.06}} \\\\  & ≈-2.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411590 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592